// WHAT SMpro WAS READ ON THIS MACHINE, kept separate from the controller and from MegaRAC.
//
// SMpro is the Ampere SoC's own out-of-band register interface (the master-write-read path into the
// RAS error registers). It belongs to the platform, not to the BMC controller: whether a machine can
// answer an SMpro read is a fact about its SoC, and it is read through a different path than any
// IPMI/Redfish control route. That is why the reading is owned here and not inside a controller
// adapter -- a MegaRAC board and an SMpro-less board are the same thing to the controller and differ
// only as platforms.
//
// The reading is re-homed here from the boot diagnostic bundle (gunbc.machine_intake
// .megarac_boot_diagnostic_bundle) because it is consumed by more than the bundle: the MegaRAC
// boot diagnostic bundle carries an SMpro field, and it must not mint the type in its own public
// surface. Both import the type from here. The raw pass observation it wraps still comes from the
// observation module this reads through.
module gunbc.host.smpro_platform_capability

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.machine_intake.ampere_smpro_observation { SmproPassObservation }

// AN SMpro READING: the pass that was taken (every probe kept whole with its raw observation,
// including when none of them was answered), or the typed fact that no pass was taken and why.
// The two arms are exhaustive and carry no more than what happened: a reading never claims a pass
// it did not take, and a not-taken fact never carries probe data that could contradict it.
type SmproReading
  = SmproProbed { observation: SmproPassObservation }
  | SmproNotTaken { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// WHY THERE IS NO CAPABILITY TYPE HERE. An earlier revision of this owner derived a platform
// capability by keying on Architecture: Aarch64 => "the platform carries SMpro". That fabricated a
// platform fact from the instruction set. SMpro is the Ampere SoC's interface, not the ARM ISA's, so
// an Aarch64 board without it (Graviton, Grace) came out as carrying SMpro -- and a machine with no
// reading came out as SmproCapabilityPassNotTaken, which asserts "the platform DOES carry SMpro and
// no pass was taken", when the truth was that it never carried it (review 81108; DESIGN §5: no
// plausible output is fabricated). The deciding fact is the SoC / platform identity, and the corpus
// has no such identity to key on yet, so nothing is inferred in its place.
//
// What this owner reports is therefore the READING: was a pass taken, with its raw observation, or
// the typed reason it was not. A consumer that genuinely needs "does this platform carry SMpro"
// must ask the platform -- a board identity, once one exists -- not the ISA. Until that fact has a
// home, an x86 or SMpro-less machine simply has no SMpro reading, which is what SmproNotTaken
// already says without the module pretending to know why.
